$DIR/main.p:38:7: the task started with set stores the per-CPU variable ticks in a task that runs later, from a value that depends on a load made here
    ticks has one copy for each processor. This is a call of set, which stores
    ticks, from a value or under a condition that depends on the load of ticks
    at main.p:37, so the task could move to another processor in the middle of
    it and read one processor's copy and write another's. The task runs later
    and on any processor, so no region held here covers its store: the task
    could not have read the value it stores from this processor's copy at the
    moment it stores it. Pass the task what it needs without loading ticks
    first, or load and store inside the task, in one region. A lone load or
    store of a whole variable of at most a machine word is allowed anywhere, as
    long as the store does not depend on a load of the same variable.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A Plutonium kernel keeps its processor's
    state in them, and an ordinary variable would be shared by every processor
    and need a lock. The compiler checks the uses instead, so that the most
    common use (a pointer to the running thread) stays safe code.
    Put the load and the store inside one region where the task cannot move:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        defer rt.EnablePreemption()
        x := ticks
        set(x)
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2022" for a longer explanation)
